WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.810
King Shark is one of the weirdest characters
in the DC Universe — and now, thanks to

00:00:04.810 --> 00:00:08.080
The Suicide Squad, he's one of the most beloved,
too.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:11.920
But who is this furious fish, and just what
keeps him swimming?

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:13.770
This is DC's King Shark explained.

00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:15.710
"You're gonna need a bigger boat."

00:00:17.009 --> 00:00:21.609
You'd probably be forgiven for thinking that
King Shark started out as an Aquaman villain.

00:00:21.609 --> 00:00:26.820
After all, it makes sense that DC's most notorious
aquatic villain would be the nemesis of DC's

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:29.150
most famous aquatic hero, right?

00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:33.410
However, although King Shark would face off
with the king of Atlantis in later issues,

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:34.770
this isn't the case.

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:40.200
He actually made his comic book debut as an
adversary of the lesser-known hero Superboy.

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:46.210
In the 1994 comic Superboy (Volume 4) #0,
King Shark is introduced in the shadows under

00:00:46.210 --> 00:00:47.810
the name of Nanaue.

00:00:47.810 --> 00:00:53.140
He subsequently stirs up trouble in the series'
ninth issue, in which he breaks out of prison,

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:58.150
has a grisly reunion with his mother, and
attempts to gobble up surfers at the beach.

00:00:58.150 --> 00:01:00.920
Predictably, Superboy himself puts a stop
to all this.

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:05.800
This cemented a long-standing rivalry between
this original version of King Shark and Superboy.

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:09.600
They would continue to face off across the
issues that followed, including one storyline

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:14.580
that sees King Shark squaring off against
his foe as a member of the Suicide Squad.

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:19.400
In the years that followed, King Shark battled
a wide array of DC Comics mainstays, including

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:20.920
Aquaman himself.

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:26.219
He also became a member of the central ensemble
in a number of Suicide Squad storylines.

00:01:26.219 --> 00:01:31.049
But his earliest stories are very much confined
to Superboy's comics — where he first established

00:01:31.049 --> 00:01:33.999
himself as a comic book baddie with serious
teeth.

00:01:34.999 --> 00:01:39.799
Some comic book characters have a very specific
origin story; one that is rarely altered,

00:01:39.799 --> 00:01:41.509
even over the course of many decades.

00:01:41.509 --> 00:01:46.109
Others, however, take a little more time to
have their origins filled out.

00:01:46.109 --> 00:01:50.600
This is true of King Shark, who was initially
presumed in his earliest Superboy appearances

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:55.420
to be either a human being who had turned
into a shark-like creature or a highly-evolved

00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:56.560
form of shark.

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:00.329
This carried over into subsequent King Shark
appearances, but it wouldn't remain that way

00:02:00.329 --> 00:02:01.390
for long.

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:05.950
Future comic book storylines made clear that
King Shark was neither mutant nor amped-up

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:06.950
shark.

00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:11.980
In Aquaman: Sword of Atlantis #40, King Shark
is confirmed to be the son of a Shark God,

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:16.540
an idea that had been introduced and dismissed
in his original Superboy appearance.

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:20.970
This didn't just clear up where this supervillain
came from, though — it also provided a nice

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:26.560
dose of humor in subsequent adventures, juxtaposing
King Shark's status of royalty with his low-life

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:28.580
thuggery in the Suicide Squad.

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:33.110
Meanwhile, the character's royal ancestry
has played a part in many of his non-comic

00:02:33.110 --> 00:02:37.750
book appearances, such as forming the basis
for his backstory on the Harley Quinn animated

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:38.750
series.

00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:40.950
Everyone knows that sharks don't talk.

00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:45.210
This simple fact is ignored by King Shark,
however, who usually displays at least some

00:02:45.210 --> 00:02:46.280
level of speech.

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:51.420
However, the various comic and media incarnations
of King Shark have given him varying degrees

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:53.250
of eloquence over the years.

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:56.620
In the early Superboy comics, he doesn't talk
at all.

00:02:56.620 --> 00:03:00.260
Subsequent comic book appearances had King
Shark be capable enough of talking that he

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:02.690
could make pointed comments and grim jokes.

00:03:02.690 --> 00:03:07.830
But later on, he was again reduced to a more
feral creature with a limited ability to speak.

00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:12.370
Modern media has taken a similarly fluid approach
to King Shark's speech patterns.

00:03:12.370 --> 00:03:16.540
For example, The Suicide Squad's version of
the character seems unable to say much other

00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:18.110
than a few choice words.

00:03:18.110 --> 00:03:22.219
Other incarnations of the character, like
that seen on the Harley Quinn animated series,

00:03:22.219 --> 00:03:26.739
are about on par with an average human being,
even displaying more emotional sensitivity

00:03:26.739 --> 00:03:29.319
than many of the people around him.

00:03:29.319 --> 00:03:34.920
"We put the profile up and we're not getting
any bites."

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:36.319
"Is that a shark joke?

00:03:36.319 --> 00:03:37.870
If so, it's very funny!"

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:42.579
The flexibility of this quality in King Shark
was certainly a total accident, but it actually

00:03:42.579 --> 00:03:46.709
has its advantages, as it allows a greater
degree of freedom for individual storytellers

00:03:46.709 --> 00:03:49.299
to put their stamp on the character.

00:03:50.299 --> 00:03:53.670
The Secret Six were a D-list group in the
DC Comics pantheon, until acclaimed writer

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:58.959
Gail Simone breathed new life into them with
her 2006 iteration of the team.

00:03:58.959 --> 00:04:03.409
This group's ever-shifting roster eventually
included none other than King Shark, who,

00:04:03.409 --> 00:04:07.219
like the others, were included in the team
as something of a last resort.

00:04:07.219 --> 00:04:11.769
Once her original plan to utilize A-list DC
Comics supervillains fell through, Simone

00:04:11.769 --> 00:04:14.430
shifted over to the lesser-known bad guys.

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:15.560
Simone once explained:

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:17.130
"It started as a joke.

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:21.280
We should just pick the crappiest villains
that no one in their right mind would want.

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:25.780
So I picked a bunch of losers too low to even
be on the Suicide Squad."

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:30.150
Though not on the initial Secret Six line-up,
it wasn't long before King Shark became a

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:34.860
prominent part of the group, appearing in
15 separate issues of Secret Six.

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:39.370
As Simone herself has noted, this more loveable
take on King Shark has served as inspiration

00:04:39.370 --> 00:04:41.730
for many subsequent versions of the character.

00:04:42.730 --> 00:04:46.069
When you're making a CW TV show, you have
to be aware that your storytelling opportunities

00:04:46.069 --> 00:04:49.060
might be limited by budgetary restrictions.

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:53.410
Even the best major broadcast TV shows are
typically made on shoestring budgets and,

00:04:53.410 --> 00:04:58.640
for a superhero series, that sadly rules out
a number of the flashier, more CGI-heavy villains

00:04:58.640 --> 00:04:59.640
out there.

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:03.780
But that hasn't stopped The Flash from delivering
some grandiose and expensive-looking foes

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:08.990
during its time on the air — including the
unexpected live-action debut of King Shark.

00:05:08.990 --> 00:05:13.990
Brought to life through full CGI, King Shark
was an unexpected presence for a major broadcast

00:05:13.990 --> 00:05:19.139
network program, but there he was, right in
the final moments of a season 2 episode.

00:05:19.139 --> 00:05:23.060
The character would go on to have a recurring
presence on The Flash that has extended well

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:24.860
beyond mere cameos.

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:28.639
In fact, King Shark has become enough of a
staple of the show to have episodes largely

00:05:28.639 --> 00:05:33.889
revolving around his own storylines, such
as the season 5 episode "King Shark vs. Gorilla

00:05:33.889 --> 00:05:34.889
Grodd."

00:05:34.889 --> 00:05:38.690
King Shark may not have been much of a Flash
adversary in the comics, but he nonetheless

00:05:38.690 --> 00:05:43.470
cemented himself as a fan-favorite villain
for the CW's take on the super-fast superhero.

00:05:44.470 --> 00:05:49.449
King Shark's presence in the virtual world
began with his debut in DC Universe Online,

00:05:49.449 --> 00:05:54.009
before making a couple of appearances in the
more family-friendly titles LEGO Batman 3:

00:05:54.009 --> 00:05:56.680
Beyond Gotham and LEGO DC Super-Villains.

00:05:56.680 --> 00:06:01.050
In all of these instances, King Shark's presence
was less a reflection on how popular the character

00:06:01.050 --> 00:06:05.880
himself was and more of an attempt to make
sure all three titles had as comprehensive

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:08.849
a library of DC Comics characters as possible.

00:06:08.849 --> 00:06:12.720
King Shark's most prominent video game appearance
has yet to arrive, however.

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:17.020
The upcoming game Suicide Squad: Kill the
Justice League will finally feature the character

00:06:17.020 --> 00:06:20.009
in a capacity beyond that of a glorified cameo.

00:06:20.009 --> 00:06:24.539
In this game, King Shark will serve as one
of the game's four leads alongside mainstays

00:06:24.539 --> 00:06:29.120
from the comic book version of the Suicide
Squad, including Harley Quinn, Captain Boomerang,

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:30.130
and Deadshot.

00:06:30.130 --> 00:06:34.750
Voiced by Samoa Joe, the trailer for the game
establishes King Shark as a more considerate

00:06:34.750 --> 00:06:39.380
soul with an extensive vocabulary, as well
as the ability to chomp down enemies in a

00:06:39.380 --> 00:06:40.380
single gulp.

00:06:41.380 --> 00:06:45.379
In recent years, King Shark has become a prominent
fixture in a number of animated DC Comics

00:06:45.379 --> 00:06:46.379
properties.

00:06:46.379 --> 00:06:50.310
While his presence in DC Super Hero Girls
demonstrated that the character can work in

00:06:50.310 --> 00:06:54.919
family-friendly animated properties, King
Shark has had a notable animated presence

00:06:54.919 --> 00:06:56.979
on shows aimed at adults.

00:06:56.979 --> 00:07:01.610
For instance, King Shark appears in the extremely
violent direct-to-video movie Batman: Assault

00:07:01.610 --> 00:07:06.270
on Arkham, in which he is voiced by John DiMaggio
— who would reprise the role in Justice

00:07:06.270 --> 00:07:08.490
League Dark: Apokolips War.

00:07:08.490 --> 00:07:12.610
Though these versions of King Shark share
a voice actor, they're quite different in

00:07:12.610 --> 00:07:16.860
personality, with the version in Apokolips
War having his dialogue largely limited to

00:07:16.860 --> 00:07:17.860
this:

00:07:17.860 --> 00:07:19.720
"King Shark is a shark."

00:07:19.720 --> 00:07:23.659
But the most popular animated incarnation
of King Shark was featured in the recent Harley

00:07:23.659 --> 00:07:24.990
Quinn series.

00:07:24.990 --> 00:07:29.120
Voiced by Ron Funches, this version of King
Shark reimagines the underwater villain as

00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:31.699
a relaxed tech-savvy everyman.

00:07:31.699 --> 00:07:35.479
First introduced in the show to help Harley
Quinn with an online profile meant to attract

00:07:35.479 --> 00:07:40.250
potential foes, King Shark ends up becoming
a prominent part of Harley's team.

00:07:40.250 --> 00:07:44.319
This version of the character became so popular
that The Suicide Squad director James Gunn

00:07:44.319 --> 00:07:46.490
even gave it a shout-out on Twitter.

00:07:46.490 --> 00:07:50.569
Speaking about the design inspiration for
his own take on King Shark, Gunn said:

00:07:50.569 --> 00:07:54.430
"I love [King Shark's] design &amp; the Harley
Quinn show &amp; @RonFunches version."

00:07:55.550 --> 00:08:01.580
The 2016 Suicide Squad movie featured plenty
of leading lights from the DC Comics mythos.

00:08:01.580 --> 00:08:05.970
A man-sized crocodile who eats people, a lady
who traps the souls of her victims in her

00:08:05.970 --> 00:08:10.090
sword, and of course, Slipknot, the man who…
can climb anything?

00:08:10.090 --> 00:08:12.230
Okay, so it wasn't a great movie.

00:08:12.230 --> 00:08:16.590
But that weird line-up would have been even
more peculiar had it included King Shark,

00:08:16.590 --> 00:08:20.010
as the movie's writer-director David Ayer
originally intended.

00:08:20.010 --> 00:08:24.520
Ayer himself revealed this in a Twitter video,
when a fan asked if there were any comic book

00:08:24.520 --> 00:08:27.330
characters he wanted to include in the film
but couldn't.

00:08:27.330 --> 00:08:29.669
King Shark turned out to be one of those figures.

00:08:29.669 --> 00:08:33.019
Of course, this isn't all that surprising,
considering the character has been a regular

00:08:33.019 --> 00:08:36.079
member of the comic book incarnation of the
team.

00:08:36.079 --> 00:08:39.169
So why didn't he get into the original Suicide
Squad movie?

00:08:39.169 --> 00:08:40.829
Well, as Ayer said:

00:08:40.829 --> 00:08:44.420
"We realized it would take a lot of work,
a lot of CG Work.

00:08:44.420 --> 00:08:47.810
I wasn't comfortable going with a fully CG
character."

00:08:47.810 --> 00:08:52.310
Hoping to keep the film as practically-realized
as possible, King Shark was left behind for

00:08:52.310 --> 00:08:54.910
the Suicide Squad's silver screen debut.

00:08:54.910 --> 00:08:59.090
Ayer also revealed that the exclusion of King
Shark allowed for the inclusion of Killer

00:08:59.090 --> 00:09:02.600
Croc, who was realized through makeup effects
rather than CGI.

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:08.110
James Gunn has always been a proponent of
motion capture technology in his comic book

00:09:08.110 --> 00:09:09.110
movies.

00:09:09.110 --> 00:09:13.019
Usually, he has a CG character represented
on set by one actor before another takes on

00:09:13.019 --> 00:09:15.440
voice-over duties in post-production.

00:09:15.440 --> 00:09:20.190
This is a method Gunn utilized for the roles
of Groot and Rocket Racoon in his two Guardians

00:09:20.190 --> 00:09:24.950
of the Galaxy movies, with the latter character
being portrayed on-set by Sean Gunn before

00:09:24.950 --> 00:09:27.060
being voiced by Bradley Cooper.

00:09:27.060 --> 00:09:31.200
Gunn has also returned to this curious method
of casting for the character of King Shark

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:32.500
in The Suicide Squad.

00:09:32.500 --> 00:09:36.750
For this incarnation of the character, Gunn
had the character represented on-set by another

00:09:36.750 --> 00:09:39.410
of Gunn's regular collaborators: Steve Agee.

00:09:39.410 --> 00:09:43.650
It was made clear early on that Agee wouldn't
be providing the voice for the character,

00:09:43.650 --> 00:09:49.040
however, since he was only credited as an
"On-Set Actor" in the DC Fandome 2020 materials

00:09:49.040 --> 00:09:50.720
for The Suicide Squad.

00:09:50.720 --> 00:09:55.050
Although he wasn't to voice King Shark himself,
Agee still scored an on-screen role in The

00:09:55.050 --> 00:09:58.920
Suicide Squad in the form of Amanda Waller's
right-hand man, John Economos.

00:10:00.060 --> 00:10:04.300
In November 2020, the internet was thrown
into a flurry of speculation when James Gunn

00:10:04.300 --> 00:10:08.440
announced that he would be reuniting with
his Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 2 cast member

00:10:08.440 --> 00:10:12.160
Sylvester Stallone for a voice-over role in
The Suicide Squad.

00:10:12.160 --> 00:10:15.870
And it wasn't long before the internet found
out who Rocky Balboa would be playing in the

00:10:15.870 --> 00:10:17.060
DC Universe.

00:10:17.060 --> 00:10:21.040
The day the first Suicide Squad trailer dropped,
it was confirmed that Stallone would be lending

00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:23.950
his distinctive voice to the character of
King Shark.

00:10:23.950 --> 00:10:27.990
The news came on the heels of speculation
not only over who Stallone would be playing,

00:10:27.990 --> 00:10:32.430
but also who could be voicing King Shark — with
frequent Gunn-collaborator Michael Rooker

00:10:32.430 --> 00:10:35.910
being a name previously rumored to provide
the character's voicework.

00:10:35.910 --> 00:10:40.170
All that speculation was for naught, however,
as Gunn had apparently long imagined Stallone

00:10:40.170 --> 00:10:41.840
inhabiting this fishy role.

00:10:41.840 --> 00:10:46.080
In fact, in the wake of the announcement,
Gunn revealed on Twitter that he wrote King

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:48.190
Shark specifically for Stallone.

00:10:48.190 --> 00:10:53.269
Turns out, there was nobody more perfect for
the role of King Shark than this icon of 1980s

00:10:53.269 --> 00:10:54.269
cinema.

00:10:54.269 --> 00:10:56.250
